  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 217 AM CST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nA finger of low stratus currently extends from southwest to eastern \nMN early this morning with more stratus in FSD's CWA. This \nadditional stratus will move northeast during this morning, covering \nmost of our CWA throughout today. Have slightly lowered today's high \ntemperatures to account for this with values now reaching the low to \nmid 50s. Southerly winds will continue with southwestern and \nsouthern MN seeing stronger values. Gusts could reach 25-30 mph \nsouth of the Minnesota River Valley. The winds are forecast to \nactually increase area-wide late tonight into Wednesday as a \nstronger low pressure system passes to our northwest. No rain is \nexpected over our CWA as we'll have dry air in place and a lack of \nsynoptic lift. But, sustained winds will reach 20-25 mph with gusts \nreaching 30-35 mph (and up to 40 mph along the Buffalo Ridge and \nextreme southern MN) Wednesday afternoon. These strong winds will \ncreate WAA and with clouds lessening, temperatures are forecast to \nrise into the mid to upper 50s. Even some lower 60s look likely \nalong the Buffalo Ridge, as we continue this warmer than normal \nFall. The winds will taper off Wednesday night into Thursday as the \nlow pressure system moves off to the northeast. But, this will drag \nthrough a cold front which will briefly cool us Thursday into \nThursday night. Mid 30s expected for lows Friday morning.\n\nThe cool down will not last long, though, as WAA commences Friday \nahead of another incoming upper-level trough moving east from the \nwestern CONUS. Highs are forecast to reach the mid 50s in central MN \nto even lower 60s in south-central MN Friday afternoon. Perhaps this \nwill be the last time we see 60 at MSP this year? The aforementioned \ntrough will eject east of the Rockies during Friday night and arrive \nin the Central Plains during Saturday. Moisture advection and \nincreasing synoptic-scale lift will bring widespread rainfall for \nthe first time in over 2 weeks. We won't see rainfall amounts like \nwe did with the system in the beginning of November but at least \n0.25\" is probable Friday night through Saturday. Temperatures will \ndrop closer to climatology Sunday and afterwards as cold air arrives \nin the wake of the system. A few snowflakes are even possible as the \nsystem wraps up Saturday night into Sunday morning. But, the best \nchances for any accumulating snow look to be well off to our north \nwhere temperatures will be colder. Model spread starts to really \nincrease as we move into early to mid-next week. But, temperatures \nlook like they will remain closer to normal with long-range guidance \nhinting at increasing chances for precipitation mid-next week.",
